# Fluorescence Enhancement in Tetracene Single Crystals

Magnetic field-dependent boost/quench analysis of triplet-triplet annihilation in tetracene, extending prior rubrene Monte Carlo simulations and benchmarking against classical Merrifield-Swenberg theory.

**Physics REU, Ivan Biaggio Research Group — Lehigh University, Summer 2026**

## Overview

Magnetic fields are known to enhance or suppress (boost/quench) fluorescence in organic semiconductor crystals through triplet-triplet annihilation processes. This effect is well documented in rubrene but had only been preliminarily explored in this group. This project extends the analysis to tetracene — a system less susceptible to competing photophysical effects, and the crystal historically used by Merrifield and Swenberg in their original treatments of the boost/quench phenomenon — making it a strong candidate for testing simulation predictions against non-simulated numerical models.

The repository contains the data processing pipeline, boost/quench curve generation, and Monte Carlo simulation code used to characterize magnetic anisotropy in tetracene single crystals.

## Background

Triplet pairs generated via singlet fission can either fuse back into a singlet exciton (boosting fluorescence) or annihilate into different spin states (quenching fluorescence), depending on the strength and orientation of an applied magnetic field relative to the crystal axes. Mapping this angular and field-strength dependence provides a probe into the underlying triplet pair spin dynamics and zero-field splitting parameters of the crystal.

## What This Project Does

- Processes time-correlated single photon counting (TCSPC) data from pulsed time-resolved photoluminescence (TDPL) measurements on tetracene, using rubrene and DPH as comparative reference systems
- Computes boost/quench ratios across field-angle and temperature combinations
- Constructs boost/quench-vs-field-strength curves for three perpendicular crystal orientations to identify saturation thresholds and detect anisotropy
- Generates a full 3D anisotropy map of triplet pair fusion kinetics from a 360° angle sweep spanning the ab-plane and c-axis
- Extends existing rubrene Monte Carlo boost/quench simulations to tetracene
- Benchmarks simulated and experimental results against classical Merrifield and Swenberg models

